WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.082
Authentication and authorization are
different controls.

2
00:00:03.162 --> 00:00:06.808
Authentication identifies the caller: a
user, service, job, or automation context.

3
00:00:06.888 --> 00:00:10.179
Authorization decides whether that caller
may perform a specific action on a

4
00:00:10.259 --> 00:00:12.740
specific resource in a specific tenant or
workflow state.

5
00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:16.162
A handler that knows the caller identity
still needs permission checks before

6
00:00:16.242 --> 00:00:17.913
returning or changing protected data.

7
00:00:17.993 --> 00:00:21.385
In Go REST APIs, trusted server-side code
should enforce sensitive decisions.

8
00:00:21.465 --> 00:00:24.098
Client-side route guards, hidden buttons,
disabled fields,

9
00:00:24.178 --> 00:00:27.520
and frontend role checks can improve
usability, but they are not enforcement.

10
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:30.536
The handler or service layer must verify
the action, object, tenant,

11
00:00:30.616 --> 00:00:31.984
and context using trusted data.

12
00:00:32.064 --> 00:00:34.089
Tenant boundaries deserve special
attention.

13
00:00:34.169 --> 00:00:36.802
A user may have access to one
organization, project, account,

14
00:00:36.882 --> 00:00:38.148
or workspace but not another.

15
00:00:38.228 --> 00:00:40.253
IDs supplied in paths, query strings,
headers,

16
00:00:40.333 --> 00:00:42.966
or request bodies should not become
authority by themselves.

17
00:00:43.046 --> 00:00:46.438
The server should connect the requested
object to the caller's permitted scope

18
00:00:46.518 --> 00:00:47.784
before using or returning it.

19
00:00:47.864 --> 00:00:49.991
Consistent authorization helpers reduce
drift.

20
00:00:50.071 --> 00:00:53.362
Shared policy functions, middleware that
attaches authenticated context,

21
00:00:53.442 --> 00:00:56.125
and service-layer checks can make
decisions more reviewable.

22
00:00:56.205 --> 00:00:59.496
Still, the authorization should be close
enough to the sensitive action that

23
00:00:59.576 --> 00:01:02.361
reviewers can see what is protected and
why the caller is allowed.

24
00:01:02.441 --> 00:01:06.036
Deny-by-default behavior keeps missing
context from becoming accidental access.

25
00:01:06.116 --> 00:01:09.407
Tests should include unauthorized users,
wrong tenants, missing identity,

26
00:01:09.487 --> 00:01:11.665
stale sessions, and service-to-service
callers.

27
00:01:11.745 --> 00:01:15.188
Access control is strongest when failure
is expected and handled deliberately.

28
00:01:15.268 --> 00:01:18.660
The authorization decision should be tied
to data loaded from trusted sources.

29
00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:20.259
If the route receives a resource ID,

30
00:01:20.339 --> 00:01:23.833
the server should confirm that resource
belongs to the allowed scope before using

31
00:01:23.913 --> 00:01:24.065
it.

32
00:01:24.145 --> 00:01:27.385
That habit keeps identity, object access,
and tenant isolation connected,

33
00:01:27.465 --> 00:01:29.440
even as routes, teams, and data models
evolve.

34
00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:31.140
Denial cases should be named in tests.
